"app-menu usage" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		App menu
		<header>An app menu is an app's top bar, joined to the top of its window or page: a start section (a menu or side bar button, then a title or breadcrumb), a middle that stays on the bar's centre, and an end section for buttons.</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Put `<ui-app-menu>` first in its page or window, outside any padding:  it runs the whole width,
			with a hairline under it.

			- Its items go in one of three sections, by their `slot`:
			  - `slot="start"`:  a menu or side bar button first, then the app's name, a title or a breadcrumb
			  - no slot:  the middle, which stays on the bar's centre (a page's modes, its tabs)
			  - `slot="end"`:  buttons, pushed to the bar's end
			- A section may hold a sub-menu (`<ui-menu slot="start">`) instead of loose items:
			  its items are the bar's all the same.
			- It is a `<ui-menu>`:  every menu attribute works (`interactive` for a menubar, `size` ...),
			  and it sends the menu's `ui-choosing`.
			- Too narrow for all three sections, the bar scrolls sideways:  it never overlaps or wraps.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			As a menu's:  Tab reaches each link and button;  with `interactive`, the bar is one Tab stop,
			and the arrow keys, Home and End move between its items.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- It's a `<nav>` landmark, or a `menubar` with `interactive`:  give it an `aria-label`.
			- An item that shows only an icon needs an `aria-label` of its own.
		</>
	</>
